m();  // drop spaces`\n3. `if (!text) return res.status(400).json({ error: \"text required\" });  // bad input`\n4. `const item = { id: Date.now(), text, done: false };`\n5. `todos.push(item);  // Create`\n6. `res.status(201).json(item);  // created`\n7. `});`\n\nIn production\nPOST /todos, validate body, insert, return 201 + row. Unique constraints. Auth required. Rate limit. The UI check is not enough — repeat validation on the server.\n\nThe browser check is a courtesy. The API repeats it. The database enforces what must stay true (unique id, not-null, foreign key).\n\nName the status code you would return on the failed path, and what you would log (request id, user id, not the password).\n\nIf this shipped as a ticket titled “Practice: POST /todos”, the acceptance test is: happy path, failed path, and a second user who must not see or change the first user’s data.\n\nFollow-up questions\nAfter “Practice: POST /todos”, a good interviewer does not stop. Be ready for these.\n\n1. What if two todos have the same title — how do you delete only one?\n\n2. Why not use the array index as id?\n\n3. What do you return on empty text?\n\n4. 201 vs 200 on create?\n\n5. Who assigns the id in production — browser or database?\n\nFor each follow-up, use rule → snippet → trap. The trap is already on this page: GET must not delete. Never trust the body for role. Hash passwords. CORS is a browser extra, not the only lock. Read the comments on the right before you paste. If a line looks clever and has no comment, it is the line that will fail in an interview. Read body.text. Push. Send.\n\nIf you cannot answer a follow-up, you memorised a heading. Change one line in the snippet and predict the new result before you run it.\n\nCompanies (Amazon, Google, Microsoft, Meta) like a 60-second version and a 3-minute version. The short one is the Summary. The long one is this page including these follow-ups.\n\nCheck yourself\nClose the answer. Keep only the question: Practice: POST /todos\